    description: "Operationalize the 'what question did we forget to ask?' discipline as a maintained doc plus trigger bake-ins, complementing the diagnostic Fundamental Concept Audit infrastructure already in place.\n\nCONTEXT (for a dev picking this up cold):\n\nThe Fundamental Concept Audit playbook (.agent/agent_playbooks_protocols_sops_skills/what-if-we-dont-know-what-the-fuck-we-are-talking-about-audit-aka-fundamental-concept-audit.md) is purely DIAGNOSTIC — it tells you what to do once you have a one-sentence suspicion (Step 1). It does NOT help when nobody suspects anything. The whole reason ADR-0023 had to be written was that the Edge.edge_type axis confusion existed in production for a long time before anyone formed a suspicion. The cadence hook (.agent/hooks/_shared/check_audit_cadence.py) addresses this partially by surfacing a reminder every ~72 development commits, but the agent still has to *pick a suspect domain* — and that requires asking 'what question have we not asked yet?', which is a different mode (generative, not diagnostic).\n\nDELIVERABLE A — docs/blind-spots.md, a standing catalog of question-SHAPES (not questions themselves):\n\nA short doc (one or two pages) listing the recurring SHAPES of questions that empirically go unasked in this codebase. The shape is the meta-pattern; the question is the instance. Format per entry:\n\n- *Shape* — one-sentence statement of the meta-pattern (e.g., 'Did we check the typing axis, not just the values?').\n- *Example* — a past instance where the shape actually fired, with PR / commit / ADR reference (e.g., ADR-0023's edge_type axis confusion, surfaced after years of accumulated drift).\n- *Trigger* — the situation that should remind you of this shape (e.g., 'whenever you're about to add a new value to a multi-value enum field').\n\nInitial seed (the ADR-0023 shape and a few likely candidates from session retrospectives):\n1. 'Did we check the typing axis, not just the values?' — ADR-0023 distinguishes axis-correctness from enumeration-completeness; see Step 3 of the audit playbook.\n2. 'What did we assume the pipeline never sees?' — assumptions about input boundaries that erode silently.\n3. 'What failure mode are we silently relying on not happening?' — graceful degradation paths that turn into load-bearing assumptions.\n4. 'What did we let the absence of evidence stand in for?' — null results being read as confirmation.\n\nMaintained by adding entries when a retrospective or incident reveals an unasked question worth keeping in the catalog. Pruned when a shape is consistently caught upstream by automation.\n\nDELIVERABLE B — trigger bake-ins so the doc actually gets consulted:\n\nReference docs/blind-spots.md from at least two of the four natural inflection points where 'what question did we forget to ask?' is the right question:\n\n- *Per-release* — scripts/prepare-release prints 'Skim docs/blind-spots.md before tagging — anything from this list relevant to the changes in this release?' and the release-workflow playbook adds the same prompt.\n- *Post-incident* — when an incident retrospective is written, the template / playbook adds a step: 'after the postmortem, decide whether the unasked question that produced this incident belongs in docs/blind-spots.md as a new shape.'\n- *Pre-refactor* — structural-fix-scope-expansion-protocol playbook (or a new pre-refactor playbook) references docs/blind-spots.md as something to skim before a major structural change locks in a frame.\n- *Domain-shift* — when a new language analyzer or framework lands, the PR description prompt or analyzer-onboarding playbook references docs/blind-spots.md and asks the contributor to note any shape-level questions reviewed.\n\nThe cadence hook is complementary, NOT a substitute. The cadence hook says 'time for an audit'; docs/blind-spots.md tells you *what to look at* when picking a suspect. Together they close the generative loop: cadence triggers the question, blind-spots names which question.\n\nACCEPTANCE:\n- docs/blind-spots.md exists with at least 3 seeded shapes (the 4 above are the floor) and a clear 'how to add an entry' preamble.\n- At least 2 of the 4 trigger bake-ins reference the doc with a concrete prompt.\n- The audit playbook adds a forward-reference to docs/blind-spots.md in its 'Cadence mechanism' section as the source of suspect-domain candidates.\n\nSIBLING ITEMS in the audit lifecycle:\n- WI-bofub-tudik-vajig-hadib-jodin-gosig-raniz-rudoj — concept-axes registry doc (done, PR #3463). The *what* that the audit nominates from.\n- WI-surih-nogis-dizut-nirim-vahut-rudol-rifir-vogor — axis-declaration ADR template. The *creation* practice for new axes.\n- WI-gusam-faful-kamum-lujon-bumip-liral-dukag-mofip — per-release axis audit cadence. A specific instance of a per-release trigger bake-in for one suspect-domain class (axes).\n- WI-tavas-voror-rakiv-litog-gatad-zonoz-holah-tugil — triage 45 emitted-but-unregistered edge_type values. The kind of accumulated drift this generative discipline would have helped surface earlier.\n\nBACKGROUND: surfaced in the audit-lifecycle session (Apr 2026). The user asked 'are we sufficiently covering our asses with this new playbook?'; the answer was 'the playbook is reactive; we need a generative complement.' The cadence hook (PR #3460), edge-type registry (PR #3459), axis-coherence linter (PR #3462), and by-axis view (PR #3463) built out the diagnostic side. This item is the generative side."  # 37c3
